{# public/includes/debugcomment.html.

   Django included this in three places: public/page.html, admin/page.html
   and public/robots.txt, the last one as
   `{% include ... with inrobotstxt=True %}` -- which switched every emoji
   to a "#" so the block was a run of robots.txt comments rather than an
   HTML comment. Every line here used to carry an
   `{% if inrobotstxt %}#{% else %}<emoji>{% endif %}` to support that.

   The port renders robots.txt from routes/public/robots.ts, which builds
   its lines in TypeScript and never touches this template, so that branch
   was unreachable in every one of its ~30 occurrences. Dropped 2026-09-05,
   maintainer decision: robots.txt does not need the debug comment. The two
   remaining includes (page.njk, wfbn/rss.njk) are both XML-ish and both
   want the HTML comment form.

   Do not write a nunjucks comment-close sequence inside a comment like
   this one: it closes the comment there and the remainder becomes output,
   which fails the precompile rather than showing up anywhere. #}
<!--
👋 {{ _("Hello") }}!

🕰️ Generated at {{ now() }}

🌐 In colo {{ colo }}
🖥️ By machine {{ instance_id }}
💾 Using code {{ version }}
{# Django's "By app" line was here, reporting the Python package of the
   view that resolved the request (gfwfbn, gfdash, gfapi2 ...). That meant
   something when those were separate Django apps with separate URL confs;
   here they are directories under routes/, every call site hardcoded the
   string its own path already told you, and no other template ever read
   app_name. Removed 2026-09-05 along with the context variable itself.

   This comment closes against the next line deliberately -- a comment that
   ends its own line leaves that trailing newline in the output, which
   showed up here as a stray blank line. #}⏱️ Took {{ render_time_ms }}ms

